NAME
     cmd -- a cmd.exe clone for Unix

SYNOPSIS
     cmd [/c|/k] [/s] [/q] [/d] [/a|/u] [/t:{bf|f}]
         [/e:{on|off}] [/f:{on|off}] [/v:{on|off}] [string]

DESCRIPTION
     cmd is a faithful reimplementation of the Windows cmd.exe command
     interpreter for Unix systems.  It provides a familiar batch scripting
     environment with the same builtin commands, pipe and redirection
     operators, and variable expansion semantics as the original.

     The project compiles to two executables:

       cmd.exe       The standard interpreter.  On startup it displays a
                     version banner and enters interactive mode.

       COMMAND.COM   Built with -DENABLE_AUTOEXEC.  On startup it looks
                     for AUTOEXEC.BAT in the current directory and runs it
                     before entering interactive mode.  This mimics the
                     behaviour of the classic MS-DOS command processor.

     Both executables support the same command-line switches and builtin
     commands listed below.

OPTIONS
     /c string       Execute string and exit.
     /k string       Execute string and remain interactive.
     /s              Special parsing mode for /c and /k (strip first and
                     last quote from the command string).
     /q              Quiet mode; do not print the banner or prompt.
     /d              Disable AutoRun scripts.
     /a              ANSI output (default).
     /u              Unicode output.
     /t:{bf|f}       Set foreground (f) and background (bf) colours as
                     hexadecimal nibbles (e.g. /t:0f for black on white).
     /e:{on|off}     Enable or disable command extensions.
     /f:{on|off}     Enable or disable file-name completion.
     /v:{on|off}     Enable or disable delayed environment variable
                     expansion.

BUILTIN COMMANDS
     ASSOC           Display or modify file extension associations.
     BREAK           Set or clear Ctrl+C checking (no-op on Unix).
     CALL            Call a batch file or a labelled subroutine.
     CD / CHDIR      Change the current directory.
     CLS             Clear the screen.
     COLOR           Set the console foreground and background colours.
     COPY            Copy one or more files.
     DATE            Display or set the system date.
     DEL / ERASE     Delete one or more files.
     DIR             List files and subdirectories.
     DOSKEY          Edit command history, create macros.
     ECHO            Display messages or toggle command echoing.
     ENDLOCAL        Restore environment variables saved by SETLOCAL.
     EXIT            Exit the interpreter or a batch file.
     FOR             Loop over a set of files or output lines.
     FTYPE           Display or modify file type open commands.
     GOTO            Jump to a label in a batch file.
     IF              Conditional execution.
     MD / MKDIR      Create a directory.
     MKLINK          Create a symbolic link or hard link.
     MOVE            Move one or more files.
     PATH            Display or set the executable search path.
     PAUSE           Wait for a key press.
     POPD            Restore the directory saved by PUSHD.
     PROMPT          Change the command prompt string.
     PUSHD           Save the current directory and change to a new one.
     RD / RMDIR      Remove a directory.
     REM             Record a comment in a batch file.
     REN / RENAME    Rename a file or directory.
     SET             Display, set, or remove environment variables.
     SETLOCAL        Save the current environment so it can be restored.
     SHIFT           Shift batch file arguments.
     START           Start a program in a new window or session.
     TIME            Display or set the system time.
     TITLE           Set the window title.
     TYPE            Display the contents of a text file.
     VER             Display the operating system version.
     VERIFY          Toggle write-verification (no-op on Unix).
     VOL             Display the volume label and serial number.

BATCH FILES
     cmd can execute batch files (.bat and .cmd extensions) with full
     support for CALL, GOTO, IF, FOR, SHIFT, %0..%9 argument expansion,
     and SETLOCAL/ENDLOCAL scoping.  Labels are defined with the colon
     prefix (e.g. :my_label) and jumped to with GOTO or CALL.

PIPES AND REDIRECTION
     cmd supports the following operators with the same precedence as
     the original cmd.exe:

       |           Pipe stdout of the left command to stdin of the right.
       < file      Redirect stdin from file.
       > file      Redirect stdout to file (overwrite).
       >> file     Redirect stdout to file (append).
       2> file     Redirect stderr to file.

     Multiple redirections can be combined on a single command line.

VARIABLE EXPANSION
     cmd expands environment variables with the %VAR% syntax.  Variable
     names are case-insensitive on Unix, matching cmd.exe semantics.
     The following special variables are always available:

       %CD%        Current directory.
       %DATE%      Current date.
       %TIME%      Current time.
       %ERRORLEVEL%  Exit code of the last command.
       %0..%9      Batch file arguments.

     Delayed expansion (%!VAR% syntax) is supported when /v:on is set.

INTERNATIONALISATION
     The user interface supports English and Simplified Chinese.  The
     active language is determined at startup from the LC_ALL,
     LC_MESSAGES, or LANG environment variables: a "zh" prefix selects
     Chinese, anything else falls back to English.

LINE EDITING
     Interactive input uses the bundled linenoise library, providing
     Emacs-style key bindings, history navigation (with persistent
     storage in ~/.cmd_history), and tab completion for file names
     and directory paths.

AUTORUN
     On startup, cmd scans the directory $PREFIX/etc/cmd/AutoRun/ for
     executable files and runs each one as a batch script (sorted by
     name).  This allows site-wide or user-specific initialisation
     without modifying AUTOEXEC.BAT.  Use the /d switch to skip AutoRun.

BUILDING

     Modern Linux (GNU Make):
       make
       make install PREFIX=/usr/local

     Generic Unix (including System V):
       sh tbuild.sh
       sh tbuild.sh CC=cc     # specify compiler
       sh tbuild.sh V=0       # quiet build

     The C source is written in C89 with no external dependencies beyond
     a POSIX.1-1990 libc.  On System V targets, pass -DLIBCMD_SYSV to
     enable the portability shims in lsysport.c, and -DLIBCMD_NO_VSNPRINTF
     if your libc lacks vsnprintf(3).  The tbuild.sh script adds these
     flags automatically.

     The following tools are helpful during development:

       sh tlint.sh    Check for files with names longer than 12 characters
                      and files missing a trailing newline.

       sh tpack.sh    Create a source archive for distribution.

FILES
     ~/.cmd_history   Command history (plain text, one command per line).
     ~/.cmd_assoc     File extension associations (ASSOC / FTYPE).
     $PREFIX/etc/cmd/AutoRun/  Scripts executed on startup.
     AUTOEXEC.BAT     Startup script for COMMAND.COM.

PORTABILITY
     cmd is tested on:

       GNU/Linux (glibc)
       System V Release 4

     The code is C89-compatible and avoids GNU extensions, designated
     initialisers, and C99 types where possible.  Old System V targets
     that lack fnmatch(3), settimeofday(2), setpriority(2), and
     open_memstream(3) are supported through the portability layer in
     lsysport.c.

